    "description": "Dasht-e Lut in southeastern Iran brings together wind-carved ridges, active dunes, stony plains, and salt landforms. Its remarkable surface heat requires careful interpretation: satellite measurements of the ground are different from weather-station measurements of air temperature. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Lut Desert", "Geomorphology", "Yardangs", "Dunes", "World Heritage"],
    "status": "Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2016",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:32:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:32:34Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Dasht-e Lut: Iran's Wind-Carved Desert | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the Lut Desert's kaluts, dunes, extreme surface heat, and the conservation questions surrounding this UNESCO World Heritage landscape."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A desert shaped by movement",
        "content": "Dasht-e Lut occupies an interior basin in southeastern Iran, where surrounding mountains contribute to exceptionally dry conditions. UNESCO inscribed its desert landscape in 2016 for natural beauty and ongoing geological processes. Its significance lies in the combination of landforms: enormous wind-carved ridges, dune fields, stony surfaces, and salt-encrusted depressions occur within the same property. [s1]\n\nThe western ridges are commonly called **kaluts**. Geomorphologists describe them as yardangs: resistant masses sculpted and streamlined by wind erosion. They are fundamentally different from dunes, which accumulate transported sand. In Lut, strong seasonal winds help explain both the removal of material and its deposition elsewhere. Extensive sand seas occupy the southern and eastern parts of the property, while stony desert surfaces provide another distinct texture. Reading these contrasts reveals an active landscape rather than a uniform expanse of sand. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "heat",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the heat records actually measure",
        "content": "Lut's reputation for extreme heat comes partly from satellite observations. NASA's account of research covering 2003–2009 reports that Lut contained the highest measured land-surface temperature in five of those years. A MODIS satellite instrument recorded 70.7 degrees Celsius there in 2005. This is a historical result from a particular analysis, not a claim that Lut holds every subsequent temperature record. [s2]\n\nLand-surface temperature describes the heated ground surface; it is not the shaded air temperature normally reported in weather forecasts. Surface materials, moisture, and vegetation influence how hot the ground becomes. Comparisons between satellite surface measurements and meteorological air-temperature records therefore require explicit qualification. The useful lesson is that Lut's exposed terrain can become extraordinarily hot, while a single headline number cannot describe the temperature of the air, the whole desert, or an entire year. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conservation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Protecting processes as well as scenery",
        "content": "The International Union for Conservation of Nature's 2016 evaluation emphasized the close juxtaposition of erosion and deposition. It also challenged the description of Lut as a place without life, recommending further investigation of its ecological and biological values. Sparse visible vegetation should not be mistaken for proof that ecological processes are absent. [s3]\n\nThat evaluation raised concerns about tourism, potential mining, and the clarity of management responsibilities. These were findings at the time of nomination, not a verified assessment of conditions in 2026. They nevertheless explain why conservation involves more than preserving a dramatic view: visitor access, development decisions, and accountable management can affect the landscape being protected. Lut is best understood as a functioning desert system whose scientific value extends beyond its most photographed ridges. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
