    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Desertification means degradation of land in arid, semi-arid, and dry sub-humid environments through climatic variations and human activities. It concerns declining land function and productivity, rather than simply the natural presence or expansion of a desert. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Desertification", "Land Degradation", "Drylands", "Soil Conservation", "Environmental Monitoring"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:32:34Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:32:34Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Desertification in Iran: Meaning and Measurement | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Learn how desertification differs from drought and natural deserts, how degradation is measured, and why restoration claims require evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "definition",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A loss of land function",
        "content": "Desertification is often imagined as a wall of sand advancing across farmland. The United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification, or UNCCD, defines a broader and more precise problem: degradation in arid, semi-arid, and dry sub-humid areas arising from climatic variations and human activities. Land can lose productivity without becoming a dune field. [s1][s2]\n\nThe convention treats land as a system encompassing soil, vegetation, other organisms, and ecological and hydrological processes. Degradation can involve wind or water erosion, deterioration of soil properties, or lasting vegetation loss. This makes the condition of a pasture or irrigated field relevant even when its surface still looks recognizably agricultural. [s1]\n\nDrought is related but distinct. In the convention's terminology, it occurs when precipitation falls significantly below normal and creates damaging hydrological imbalances. Desertification concerns degradation of the land system. The two can interact, but a dry year alone does not establish a persistent decline in land function. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "drivers-and-response",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why management and livelihoods matter",
        "content": "UNCCD identifies overcultivation, excessive grazing, deforestation, and poor irrigation practices among the human pressures associated with desertification. Its explanation also connects these pressures to social and economic circumstances. Understanding degradation therefore requires asking how people obtain food, fuel, income, and access to land, as well as examining rainfall and vegetation. [s2]\n\nThe response is correspondingly broader than planting trees. Measures described by UNCCD include erosion prevention, improved water management, sustainable pasture management, windbreaks, and drought early warning. The convention distinguishes preventing degradation, rehabilitating partly degraded land, and reclaiming desertified land. These are different objectives and should not be treated as interchangeable evidence of success. [s1][s2]\n\nIran provides a concrete example of why integrated approaches are proposed. UNDP's Hamoun project summary links wetland degradation to climate change and unsustainable water use, and describes ecosystem restoration alongside alternative livelihoods as project objectives. That statement establishes the intended approach, not a demonstrated outcome or a national explanation applicable to every Iranian dryland. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a degradation statistic can establish",
        "content": "UN Sustainable Development Goal indicator 15.3.1 measures degraded land as a proportion of total land area. UNEP explains that its assessment uses land cover, land productivity, and carbon stocks. Under its 'One-Out All-Out' approach, a significant negative change in any one of these components can identify degradation. This is a defined assessment method, not a direct count of expanding sand dunes. [s3]\n\nUNEP describes national collection and validation through the UNCCD reporting process on a four-year cycle. A reporting year consequently should not be mistaken for a complete annual sequence of observations. Comparing percentages also requires checking their geographical coverage, assessment periods, and methods. A national land-degradation indicator and a local dust measurement answer different questions. [s3]\n\nFor readers assessing an Iranian restoration claim, the useful questions are specific: what land was assessed, what changed, over which period, and by which measure? An intervention's name or announced objective cannot answer them. Keeping natural desert landscapes, temporary drought, and persistent degradation conceptually separate makes both environmental reporting and conservation priorities easier to understand. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
